WEBVTT

00:00:00.601 --> 00:00:04.051
Here's a small app built with lit-html.

00:00:04.051 --> 00:00:07.380
A number, and a button that adds one to it.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:13.729
Open the browser's developer tools, and you'll find a few things in the DOM that you didn't write.

00:00:13.729 --> 00:00:15.391
An empty comment.

00:00:15.391 --> 00:00:22.711
And a comment with a strange name: lit, and a long random number, between dollar signs.

00:00:22.711 --> 00:00:24.374
Where do they come from?

00:00:24.374 --> 00:00:33.189
Let's follow one template all the way through lit-html, from the code you write, to the moment the number on the screen changes.

00:00:35.671 --> 00:00:37.113
Here's the code.

00:00:37.113 --> 00:00:47.731
The counter function takes a number, and returns a template with three expressions in it: the span's class, the number itself, and a click handler.

00:00:47.731 --> 00:00:50.219
We render it once with zero.

00:00:50.219 --> 00:00:54.311
And each click renders it again, with the next number.

00:00:54.311 --> 00:01:02.703
Inside lit-html, rendering has three phases: prepare, create, and update.

00:01:02.703 --> 00:01:08.670
Much of what makes lit-html fast is how often it gets to skip the first two.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.802
Let's start with the template itself.

00:01:10.802 --> 00:01:16.648
The word html, right before the backtick, makes this a tagged template literal.

00:01:16.648 --> 00:01:25.577
When it runs, JavaScript splits it into two lists: the fixed strings of HTML, and the values of the expressions in between.

00:01:25.577 --> 00:01:28.800
Then it calls the html function, with both.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.144
And html does almost nothing with them.

00:01:32.144 --> 00:01:39.593
It returns a plain object, holding the strings, the values, and a number that says this is HTML.

00:01:39.593 --> 00:01:42.416
That object is called a TemplateResult.

00:01:42.416 --> 00:01:45.801
Nothing has been parsed, and the DOM hasn't been touched.

00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:49.348
Now, call counter again, with one.

00:01:49.348 --> 00:01:50.771
The values are new.

00:01:50.771 --> 00:01:52.915
But the strings array isn't.

00:01:52.915 --> 00:01:55.810
It's the very same object as before.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:02:04.102
JavaScript makes one strings array for each template literal in your code, and passes that same array every time the literal runs.

00:02:04.102 --> 00:02:07.787
So the array itself makes a perfect cache key.

00:02:07.787 --> 00:02:10.890
Same strings array, same template.

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:12.812
Now we render.

00:02:12.812 --> 00:02:18.458
First, render checks the container for a part it left there on an earlier render.

00:02:18.458 --> 00:02:21.721
This is the first render, so there isn't one.

00:02:21.721 --> 00:02:28.768
So it makes one: a child part, and it marks where that part begins with an empty comment.

00:02:28.768 --> 00:02:30.950
That's the first mystery comment.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:40.003
A child part manages a spot in the DOM that can hold content: text, nodes, a list, or a whole template.

00:02:40.003 --> 00:02:45.560
It's stored on the container, for next time, and then it's given our TemplateResult.

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:49.044
The first thing the part does is look up the template.

00:02:49.044 --> 00:02:54.549
lit-html keeps a cache of prepared templates, keyed by the strings array.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:57.932
This one has never been rendered, so it isn't there.

00:02:57.932 --> 00:03:03.958
In fact, until this moment, lit-html hasn't looked at this template's HTML at all.

00:03:03.958 --> 00:03:06.610
Now it has to prepare it.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:10.213
Preparing turns the strings into DOM.

00:03:10.213 --> 00:03:13.716
lit-html doesn't include an HTML parser.

00:03:13.716 --> 00:03:19.220
It uses the browser's, which is fast, and handles every corner of HTML.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:24.705
But a parser needs complete HTML, and our strings have holes in them.

00:03:24.705 --> 00:03:31.520
So first, lit-html fills each hole with a marker, one it can find again after parsing.

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:35.405
Which marker to use depends on where the hole is.

00:03:35.405 --> 00:03:40.430
So lit-html scans the strings, but it doesn't build anything.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:47.177
It tracks just enough to know whether it's in text, inside a tag, or inside an attribute's value.

00:03:47.177 --> 00:03:50.160
A handful of regular expressions is all that takes.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.145
The first hole is in the value of the class attribute.

00:03:54.145 --> 00:04:01.153
lit-html puts the marker in the value, and adds $lit$ to the attribute's name.

00:04:01.153 --> 00:04:08.230
That makes the binding easy to find later, and the browser never sees a real class attribute full of marker text.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.915
The second hole is in text, inside the span.

00:04:11.915 --> 00:04:16.500
Here, the marker goes between angle brackets, after a question mark.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:22.127
That's the syntax for a processing instruction, but the parser turns this one into a comment.

00:04:22.127 --> 00:04:25.121
It's a few bytes shorter than writing out a comment.

00:04:25.121 --> 00:04:27.649
The third hole is the event binding.

00:04:27.649 --> 00:04:31.440
That's another attribute value, so it's handled just like the first.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:37.530
The marker itself is the word lit, and a random number, between dollar signs.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:45.300
The number is picked once, when the page loads, so it's very unlikely to appear in your templates by accident.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:51.269
With the markers in, the strings join into one complete piece of HTML.

00:04:51.269 --> 00:04:57.337
lit-html creates a template element, and sets its innerHTML to that string.

00:04:57.337 --> 00:04:59.601
The browser parses it into DOM.

00:04:59.601 --> 00:05:03.126
The contents of a template element are inert.

00:05:03.126 --> 00:05:08.415
Scripts don't run, images don't load, and custom elements don't upgrade.

00:05:08.415 --> 00:05:12.159
It's DOM that's only there to be copied.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:18.210
Next, lit-html walks the new DOM once, counting nodes as it goes.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:25.270
It only visits elements and comments, so text nodes, like all of this whitespace, aren't counted.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:29.637
Node zero is the span, and it has a bound attribute.

00:05:29.637 --> 00:05:36.547
So lit-html records a template part: an attribute part named class, at node zero.

00:05:36.547 --> 00:05:38.841
Then it removes the attribute.

00:05:38.841 --> 00:05:41.367
Node one is the marker comment.

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:44.274
That's a child part, at node one.

00:05:44.274 --> 00:05:45.657
The comment stays.

00:05:45.657 --> 00:05:47.230
It'll be needed later.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:49.253
Node two is the button.

00:05:49.253 --> 00:05:55.120
Its bound attribute starts with an at sign, so it's an event part, for click.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:06:01.028
A dot would make it a property part, and a question mark, a boolean attribute part.

00:06:01.028 --> 00:06:03.211
Recorded, and removed.

00:06:03.211 --> 00:06:08.240
Three holes, three template parts, so the walk can stop.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:17.350
The template element and its list of parts together make a Template, and it goes into the cache, under the strings array.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:19.012
That's the prepare phase.

00:06:19.012 --> 00:06:26.720
It runs once for each template literal in your code, no matter how many times, or in how many places, you render it.

00:06:26.771 --> 00:06:28.093
Back in the root part.

00:06:28.093 --> 00:06:31.280
Is it already showing an instance of this template?

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:32.844
No, it's empty.

00:06:32.844 --> 00:06:36.011
So it's time for phase two: create.

00:06:36.011 --> 00:06:42.923
lit-html makes a TemplateInstance, and clones the template's content, with importNode.

00:06:42.923 --> 00:06:44.165
That's fast.

00:06:44.165 --> 00:06:47.460
The browser copies the nodes without parsing anything.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:54.287
Then it walks the copy, counting nodes the same way, and stops at each index in the parts list.

00:06:54.287 --> 00:07:00.293
There, it creates a part: a small object that holds a direct reference to that node.

00:07:00.293 --> 00:07:02.496
An attribute part for the span.

00:07:02.496 --> 00:07:05.018
A child part that starts at the comment.

00:07:05.018 --> 00:07:07.081
And an event part for the button.

00:07:07.081 --> 00:07:12.598
From now on, lit-html never has to search for these places again.

00:07:12.621 --> 00:07:15.609
Now, phase three: update.

00:07:15.609 --> 00:07:19.831
The instance gives each of its parts the matching value.

00:07:19.831 --> 00:07:22.918
The attribute part gets an empty string.

00:07:22.918 --> 00:07:28.471
Its last value was nothing, so this is a change, and it sets the class attribute.

00:07:28.471 --> 00:07:30.597
The child part gets zero.

00:07:30.597 --> 00:07:34.980
It creates a text node, and inserts it right after its comment.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.825
The event part gets the click handler.

00:07:37.825 --> 00:07:43.393
It adds an event listener, but the listener it adds is itself.

00:07:43.393 --> 00:07:48.441
When a click comes in, the part calls whichever function it was given most recently.

00:07:48.441 --> 00:07:52.809
All of this happens while the copy is still detached from the page.

00:07:52.809 --> 00:07:58.621
Then the root part inserts it, all at once, and remembers which instance it's showing.

00:07:58.621 --> 00:08:01.387
And there's the second mystery comment.

00:08:01.387 --> 00:08:08.099
It's the marker from the template, copied into the page, where it marks the start of the child part.

00:08:08.101 --> 00:08:09.924
Now, click the button.

00:08:09.924 --> 00:08:17.570
counter runs with one, and makes a new TemplateResult: the same strings array, with new values.

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:20.495
render finds the part it left on the body.

00:08:20.495 --> 00:08:25.223
The template is already in the cache, and the part is already showing an instance of it.

00:08:25.223 --> 00:08:28.148
So prepare and create are both skipped.

00:08:28.148 --> 00:08:29.500
Straight to update.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:32.563
The class changed, from empty to odd.

00:08:32.563 --> 00:08:35.226
That's one call to setAttribute.

00:08:35.226 --> 00:08:40.231
The number changed too, but lit-html doesn't make a new text node.

00:08:40.231 --> 00:08:43.454
It changes the text of the one it made last time.

00:08:43.454 --> 00:08:47.818
And the click handler is a new function, so the part just keeps it.

00:08:47.818 --> 00:08:50.200
The listener on the button stays the same.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.865
That's two small writes to the DOM.

00:08:52.865 --> 00:08:56.391
Nothing else in the template is touched, or even looked at.

00:08:56.391 --> 00:09:02.559
And when a string or a number hasn't changed, its part skips the DOM entirely.

00:09:02.561 --> 00:09:05.846
A child part can hold a whole template, too.

00:09:05.846 --> 00:09:13.870
It handles one exactly the way the root part did: it looks up the template, creates an instance if it needs one, and updates it.

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:20.797
If the part gets a different template than last time, it clears out what's there, and creates a new instance.

00:09:20.797 --> 00:09:27.604
Switching back skips prepare, since that template is in the cache, but it does create the instance again.

00:09:27.604 --> 00:09:33.830
If you switch back and forth a lot, the cache directive keeps both instances around.

00:09:33.861 --> 00:09:41.596
Give a child part an array, and it makes a child part for each item, each one marked by a pair of empty comments.

00:09:41.596 --> 00:09:44.151
That's where the rest of the empty comments come from.

00:09:44.151 --> 00:09:48.121
Here, three list items come from one template literal.

00:09:48.121 --> 00:09:51.840
It's prepared once, and each item gets its own instance.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:59.311
On the next render, items are matched up by position, and each instance is updated in place.

00:09:59.311 --> 00:10:06.780
To match items by a key instead, so their DOM moves when the list is reordered, use the repeat directive.

00:10:06.861 --> 00:10:08.933
So that's the whole trip.

00:10:08.933 --> 00:10:10.730
Let's stack up the caching.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:15.734
JavaScript gives each template literal one strings array.

00:10:15.734 --> 00:10:23.559
lit-html uses that array as a key, so each template is scanned and parsed only once.

00:10:23.559 --> 00:10:31.725
Each child part remembers the instance it's showing, so DOM is only cloned when the template in that spot changes.

00:10:31.725 --> 00:10:38.820
And each part remembers its last value, so an unchanged string or number never touches the DOM.

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:40.662
And those comments?

00:10:40.662 --> 00:10:48.711
The empty ones are markers that lit-html creates as it renders: for the root part, and for list items.

00:10:48.711 --> 00:10:54.537
The ones with lit and a number are the template's markers, copied into the page.

00:10:54.537 --> 00:10:57.820
Each one marks where a child part starts.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:06.715
For more detail, read Life of a lit-html render, in the lit repository, and the source, lit-html.ts.

00:11:06.715 --> 00:11:10.420
It's well commented, and it's all in one file.

